VCEncTest - the vcenc encoder on the Raspberry Pi
=================================================

vcenc is new: the VideoCore's H.264 encoder (the firmware's
ril.video_encode), reached through RISC OS's VCHIQ module and MMAL in the
same way vcdec reaches the decoder. It is in libvcdec beside vcdec. This is
its first run on a real Pi: until now it has only been tested against a
fake encoder that behaves the way Linux's driver expects the firmware to.

VCEncTest drives it as a program would. It makes test pictures (a pattern
that moves), sends them until vcenc says the encoder's input is full, then
takes every packet that's ready. It checks that the stream's SPS and PPS
come first, that the packets come in order with their times, and where the
keyframes are. With -d it then decodes the stream again on the VideoCore
(with vcdec) and compares each picture with the one sent (PSNR, in dB:
higher is closer; around 35 or more is good, under 25 counts as a fault).

The encoder needs gpu_mem=128 or more in CONFIG/TXT for 1080p (as the
decoder does). VCEncTest says how much the VideoCore has at the start of
each run.

Running it (please send back Result, ResultO, ResultT and ResultV):
  Verbose  Run this first. 320x180, 10 pictures, with every MMAL message
           in the report: how the firmware answers the ports, the
           settings and the buffers. If anything else fails, this shows
           why. Writes ResultV.
  Test     640x360, 1280x720 and 1920x1080, 60 pictures each, encoded and
           decoded again. Keeps two of the streams (Stream360 and
           Stream1080, H.264 "Annex B": VLC or ffplay can play them).
           Writes Result.
  Options  640x360 with each of vcenc's settings in turn: Main, Baseline,
           SPS and PPS before every keyframe, constant bit rate, a
           keyframe asked for, PCI memory, 4 input buffers, quantiser
           limits, level 4.0. Each stream decoded again. Writes ResultO.
  Speed    How fast it encodes: 720p and 1080p, 300 pictures each, not
           checked; 1080p from PCI memory and with 4 input buffers too.
           Writes ResultT.

Each run ends with "Result: OK - ..." or what went wrong.

What the reports tell us:
  - "CONFIG packet": the SPS and PPS the encoder made, and its profile
    and level (profile_idc 100 is High, 77 Main, 66 Baseline).
  - "Encoded N pictures in ... a second": the encoder's speed with
    pictures already made (the time to make them isn't counted).
  - "Copying pictures in": the time spent copying the pictures into the
    encoder's buffers.
  - "Decoded again ... PSNR": how close the decoded pictures are.
  - The page at &8000 line: "MOVED" is a failure (restart before the
    next test).

Options (for running it by hand):
  vcenctest [-o report] [-w stream] [-v] [-d] [-t] [-s WxH] [-n frames]
            [-r fps] [-b bitrate] [-g gop] [-p high|main|baseline]
            [-l level] [-i] [-c] [-k N] [-q min,max] [-B n] [-m pci] [-G]
  -o file   add the report to file          -w file   the stream to file
  -v        every MMAL message               -d        decode again, compare
  -t        a shorter report                 -s WxH    the size (640x360)
  -n N      pictures (60)                    -r fps    frames a second (25)
  -b bits   bits a second (10000000)         -g N      a keyframe every N
  -p name   profile (high)                   -l level  e.g. 41 for 4.1
  -i        SPS and PPS before every keyframe
  -c        constant bit rate                -k N      a keyframe at picture N
  -q a,b    quantiser limits                 -B n      input buffers (2, up to 4)
  -m pci    PCI memory, not a buffer pool    -G        ignore gpu_mem

Needs SharedUnixLibrary and ARMEABISupport (from PackMan), RISC OS 5.28
or later on a Raspberry Pi.

